course: everyday
lesson: 403
title: Copying game moves on a little brother
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Noah had been watching fighting games at Theo's, and in the living room he decided to try a flying kick on his little brother Alfie, who is six. In the game, the other fighter flies across the screen, bounces once and jumps straight back up with a cheerful «ROUND TWO». Alfie flew into the coffee table, split his lip and screamed the house down. There was no health bar. There was a trip to the minor injuries unit, a mum who didn't speak to Noah for the whole car journey, and no console for a month.
explain: Alfie, meanwhile, had learned something too: the sound effects. On Monday he stood on a chair at nursery and bellowed «FINISH HIM!» at a boy holding a toy dinosaur. The nursery teacher rang home. Mum looked at Noah. Noah looked at the floor. A week later he found a much better idea: he taught Alfie a «secret code» for the game's menu — up, up, down, star — which does absolutely nothing, but Alfie types it in with enormous pride every time.
explain: It was the same story elsewhere. Kai showed his cousin «the special grab» and it actually hurt — the cousin cried and the visit ended early. Ollie demonstrated a «combo» on the sofa and his little sister got an elbow in the eye, which was purple for a week. Ruby's cousin copied a game finishing move on a classmate at school, and the classmate fell and banged his head; the cousin was excluded for two days and the other boy's parents were furious.
explain: Jayden said the thing everyone half believed: «Real fights are like games, you just get up again with full health.» They aren't. Real bodies bruise, bones break, heads that hit floors can be badly hurt, and nothing resets. And the person hurt is often the youngest and smallest in the room — the one who trusted you.
explain: Some boys found the funny version. Theo and Leo do slow-motion «film stunt» fights with no touching at all, a metre apart, with dramatic noises, like actors rehearsing. Leo «freezes» his brother with an ice move, and his brother freezes like a statue, wobbling, until he can't stop laughing. When Zara's brother wanted to practise game moves on the trampoline, Zara said, «No moves. Bouncing contest instead,» and he went for it. Games are for the screen; the fun bit off-screen is the pretending.
fact: In games characters get up again with full health, but real people do not.
fact: Moves copied from games can cause real injuries such as cuts, black eyes, broken bones and head injuries.
fact: A hit on the head, or a fall that bangs the head, can be serious and needs an adult's attention.
fact: Younger brothers and sisters are smaller and more easily hurt, and they trust older children.
fact: Little children copy what older children say and do, including shouting phrases from games.
fact: Hurting someone at school by copying a game move can lead to serious trouble, such as being excluded.
fact: Stage and film fights are faked by trained people, using careful tricks and camera angles, so that nobody gets hurt.
fact: Make-believe play with no touching, like freezing or pretend battles, can be just as much fun.
fact: Trampolines cause many injuries, and playing rough on them makes accidents much more likely.
fact: When someone is hurt, the right thing is to stop at once, help them and get an adult.
exercise: What happens in the game after the flying kick? | The fighter bounces and gets straight back up | The fighter goes to hospital and misses the next level | The game stops forever | The fighter cries for his mum
exercise: What happened to Alfie in real life? | He hit the coffee table and split his lip | He bounced off the cushions and got straight up | He laughed and asked for more | Nothing at all
exercise: Why are moves from games dangerous in real life? | Real bodies get hurt and nothing resets | Real people are made of pixels too, underneath | They only work with a controller in your hand | They are too slow to hurt anyone
exercise: What did Alfie copy at nursery? | The game's shouting | The flying kick | The secret code | The ice move
exercise: Why do little children copy older children? | They look up to them and learn by copying | They are made to by their parents | They want to annoy them on purpose | They never copy anyone
exercise: What was Noah's better idea for Alfie? | A secret code that does nothing but feels special | Teaching him a gentler kick that only hurts a bit | Letting him play the 18 game | Sending him to his room
exercise: What happened when Kai showed his cousin «the special grab»? | It hurt and the visit ended early | The cousin won the next match easily | Everyone clapped | Nothing, grabs are harmless
exercise: Why is a little sister more likely to get hurt than a friend your age? | She is smaller and trusts you | She is made of softer stuff than you | Little children never really get hurt | Friends are always more fragile
exercise: What happened to Ruby's cousin after copying a finishing move at school? | He was excluded and the other boy was hurt | He got a prize for doing a good impression in assembly | Nothing, it was only a game | The other boy thanked him
exercise: Jayden says real fights are like games. What's the truth? | Real injuries stay and can be serious | Yes, people get full health back after a rest | Yes, if you wait a minute | Only in some countries
exercise: How do Theo and Leo make their fake fights safe? | They never touch and stay apart, like stunt actors | They wear thick gloves and hit softly | They only fight on the bed | They take turns hitting each other
exercise: Why is the freezing game good fun? | It's pretend, so everyone laughs and nobody's hurt | Because Leo always wins every time | Because it hurts a little bit | Because the little brother has to obey
exercise: What did Zara suggest instead of game moves on the trampoline? | A bouncing contest | Doing the moves more carefully | Moving the trampoline indoors | Trying the moves on grass
exercise: Alfie is crying and his lip is bleeding. What should Noah do first? | Stop, help him and get an adult | Say it was all Alfie's own fault | Pretend he didn't see anything happen | Tell him to get up like in the game
exercise: What kind of courage did Noah need after Alfie got hurt? | To own up and say sorry to Alfie and Mum | To do the kick again, but better | To hide the console | To blame the game company for showing him
exercise: Which is the best way to play «fighters» with a younger brother? | Pretend from a distance with silly sound effects | Show him the real moves slowly | Let him hit you first | Only use the moves that don't hurt very much
test: «go on show us the flying kick on ur brother, he can take it». What's the wise answer? | No, he could get hurt | Yes, if he lands on the sofa | Yes, he's tough, he's a boy | Only a small one, for fun
test: «in the game he just gets back up lol». So will a real person? | No, real people stay hurt and nothing resets | Yes, after a few seconds they're fine | Yes, if they're young | Only if you say «round two»
test: «alfie shouted FINISH HIM at nursery haha». Why did the teacher ring home? | Little kids copy older ones, so she wanted to know where it came from | Shouting is against the rules at nursery | Alfie had won a prize | The dinosaur was broken
test: «grab's harmless, watch» — and the cousin's crying. What now? | Stop at once, check she's ok and get an adult | Do it again but softer | Tell her to stop being such a baby about a little knock | Leave before anyone notices
test: «ollie's sister's got a purple eye, said she walked into a door». What really happened? | She got an elbow while he showed a combo | She really did walk into a door at home, like she said | She was born with it | She did it herself for fun
test: «just copy the finishing move on him at school, it's funny». What's likely to happen? | Someone gets hurt and you get in serious trouble | Everyone laughs and nothing much happens to anyone | The teacher joins in | You get famous
test: «stunt fights in films are real hits innit». True? | No, they're faked by trained people so nobody gets hurt | Yes, actors really do get hit, but they're trained to be tough | Yes, that's why they're paid so much money | Only in old films
test: «bro wants to do game moves on the trampoline, sound?» Best reply? | No, have a bouncing contest instead | Yes, trampolines are soft, so nobody can get hurt | Yes, but only the kicks | Only if mum's not watching
test: «leo froze his brother with an ice move and he's STILL a statue lol». Why is this fine? | It's make-believe with no touching | Because the brother is older and bigger | Because ice moves are weaker than kicks | It isn't, freezing someone really hurts them
test: Mum: «Noah, what were you thinking?!» What's the best answer? | «I'm sorry, I copied a game and he got hurt» | «It's the game's fault, it shows you how to do it» | «Alfie fell over by himself» | «He asked me to do it»
test: «real fights r just like Tekken». Teacher's answer? | No, real people bruise, break bones and don't reset | Yes, real people have health bars too, you just can't see them | Yes, but slower | No, real fights are always harmless
exam: Ascertain the principal distinction between injuries sustained by a character in a video game and those sustained by a person. | Injuries to a person persist and may be grave, whereas a character is restored | Injuries to a person, as to a character, disappear upon the commencement of a new round | There is no distinction of any significance | A character's injuries are of greater severity
exam: Identify the course of action required of an older child whose demonstration of a move has injured a younger sibling. | To cease immediately, render assistance and summon an adult | To repeat the demonstration with greater care | To attribute the injury to the younger sibling | To conceal the occurrence from all adults
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the conduct of young children in respect of the words and actions of older children? | Young children are disposed to imitate them | Young children invariably disregard them entirely | Young children imitate only their own parents | Young children imitate solely that which is harmless
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning fights depicted in films and on the stage. | They are simulated by trained performers so that no person is injured | They involve genuine blows delivered with full force | They are performed by untrained volunteers | They invariably result in injury to the performers
